ce:section.ce:para:
The High-resolution mass spectrometry (HRMS) coupled to liquid chromatography analysis revealed the formation of five TPs during the application of solar/PS process. All the HRMS data (ion molecular formula, m/z [M-H]-, mass error (ppm) and double bond equivalent (DBE)) have been compiled in Table 3
                        . Based on the identified TPs and considering that all of them were generated during the first steps of the process, three transformation pathways can be proposed. The first pathway, that was found to be the main one, based on the area of TP1 and TP2, includes radicals’ formation and subsequent coupling leading to dimers with ether and biphenyl structures. In general, dimerization reactions begin with an electron transfer and H+ abstraction that leads to the formation of the corresponding radicals (Fig. 7
                        ), which can further react via C–O and C–C coupling (Frontistis et al., 2017).

A relatively high concentration of BPS (3 mg L
                           −1
                        ) was used in this study to simulate potential accumulation effects of BPS and its transformation products in aquatic environments, particularly under conditions where the treatment process may not fully remove total organic carbon (TOC). This choice is further supported by reports indicating a rising trend in BPS concentrations in surface waters over the years, with levels reaching up to 1600 ng/L by 2016 in Taihu Lake (Qiu et al., 2019; Pant et al., 2023). Such findings highlight the need to consider worst-case scenarios in the assessment of environmental risks associated with BPS.
